## Note

In this article I simply make a critique of going into debt to study. The university will give you some job and professional opportunities that you will not be able to find without a degree. However, in my personal opinion, if you want to dedicate yourself to making websites and some APIs, a university degree is not necessary. Other jobs such as scientific software and data analysis require greater academic rigor. The truth is that everything depends on where you want to work. You must clearly define what you want to do and determine whether a university degree (and the university you will study at) will help you reach that goal. In any case, going into debt to study can be a tremendous burden after leaving formal studies, make sure you can pay for your education before taking on debt.

## Introduction

Nowadays studying a degree is extremely expensive and if you use a loan like the CAE, for a 5 to 7 year degree you will probably be in debt for at least 20 years, paying a monthly installment of between 100 and 150 thousand pesos (CLP) (besides, it goes up every month since it is in UF). You will owe around 40 million pesos.
If you don't pay the installment, you risk your guarantor (your father, mother or some relative) being sued and having a thousand and one problems.

## What to do?

If you like technology, science and computer science in general, I recommend the following:

Study something quick (minimum 1 year and ideally 2 years) at a professional institute, an online course or a tech community and get a well-paid job (over a million a month).
With this you will have no problem paying for an Engineering degree (around 5 million per year) at a good University (if you still want to enter the university world).

> Note: While earning over a million is possible, it requires a lot of effort. In any case, you can get a job with better salary and working conditions than working in other areas (retail, customer service, others). You can also do freelance work to get occasional money.

Don't enroll in a University unless you have enough money to pay the annual costs without resorting to loans. The worst thing you can do is go into debt without knowing if you will be able to pay the installment. One of the worst situations you can be in is knowing that you are in debt and you cannot pay to get out of that debt quickly. Having a university degree does not guarantee that you will have a good salary that allows you to pay off the debt in a short time.

## How to get a well-paid job?

First, if you are from the regions you have to go to Santiago or Remote.

Unfortunately in Chile remote work is scarce and regional companies
usually pay very little.

Most of the IT companies are in Santiago and offer good salaries even for people who don't have a university degree.

Be careful with Startups. They usually pay little, are unstable and probably die within the first 5 years (them offering you shares to justify the low salary is a scam, 1% of 0 is 0. Also the supposed benefits like coffee, beers or video games are not rare in IT companies). Maybe you should try your own venture; if you have no money, don't think that Corfo or another SME fund will help you. Winning a Corfo doesn't guarantee you anything and the prizes are usually so meager that they only let you function for a couple of months. Starting a business in Chile is only possible if you have sufficient capital. SMEs are not a good alternative if you want to earn money and don't have the backing to survive the first 5 years.

Go to hackathons. Only go if you are looking to make contacts and maybe a job offer.

The ideal is to go to companies that pay you over a million pesos and that have more than 3 years of existence, with good recommendations.

## Meat Grinders

A meat grinder is a subcontracting company. Normally the company sells your time to others. For example they sell you as a Java expert (charging as a Senior) while you have only done a couple of tutorials (they pay you as a Junior), creating a tremendous level of stress. There are several companies of this type in the world and in Chile too, so always get advice and research before entering a company.

## Agencies

There are good agencies and bad agencies. An agency basically accepts diverse jobs and various projects, normally for some client who wants to put together an advertising campaign or some related project. The main problem with agencies is their short deadlines and the low quality of the development. If they don't have a solid way of working with tools, processes and adequate methodology, you will end up in a great pit of stress.

## Contract

Many times when doing freelance work or even full-time work, no contract is made. This is dangerous for you because they can abuse you, not pay you or simply be a problem. Make sure you have a contract where the amount they will pay you, the date, the benefits and anything important are clearly established. Look for a person with more work experience to advise you before signing any document. There are many characters who like to abuse young people with leonine clauses.

## Recommendations

Look for a company that has one or two established products and that has some fame in the market. Normally it can be a well-known mobile application. People are always needed for internal projects that help improve the processes that support that application.

If you need guidance on where to get a decent job at a technology company in Chile, you can ask the friends of the [Devs Chile](https://devschile.cl/) community, `#pegas` channel.

Do work for NGOs and other social aid institutions. You will be able to contribute with technology in these organizations and you will get material for your portfolio and experience in the trenches. Maybe you earn little or no money but at least you will be contributing to a higher purpose. But never agree to work for free for companies that make a profit, that is abuse.

Practice by building personal projects, maybe you can put together a new service or product that you can sell and earn a couple of extra chauchas (Chilean slang for small money). This will allow you to experiment with new technologies and it will be much easier for you to learn without time or budget limits. Building projects for fun will allow you to discover new skills or ideas that mark your path.

Finally, focus on building a portfolio of projects that highlights your skills. Show the best of your repertoire and you will surely be better regarded than a person with a degree but with nothing to show of real experience.

## Recommended Skills

### English

First you have to learn English. If your written and spoken English level is poor, you must improve it as soon as possible. Try to get a TOEFL or IELTS certification and with a Work and Travel you can go to a country like Canada, Australia or the United States where they will pay you much more than in Chile and without the need for a university degree.

English is also the language of the best texts, courses and programming languages. It is indispensable for any computer science professional.

Having a good level of English you could even get a remote job at a foreign company, which would allow you to work from any region of Chile, eliminating the innate stress of Santiago.

### A Good Computer and Equipment

You can do most of the development on a normal computer but the benefit of the Mac is that you can develop for the three platforms: Windows, Linux and Mac. The Macbook Air is the best alternative to start with, at a reasonable cost to have an adequate development environment. Later you can upgrade the equipment when you have a decent salary. Having a good development environment is as important as having a good chair.

You can always buy second hand at a good price. I recommend models from 2017 onwards. Never buy a Mac older than 5 years because it has the danger of not having software updates. A decent alternative is a Mac Mini.

Recommended specs: Minimum 8 GB of RAM and 256 of hard drive, 2017+.

Tip: Check the number of cycles. The fewer cycles the better. [Mac cycles](https://support.apple.com/es-lamr/HT201585). Maximum count for MacBook Air: 1000 cycles.

### Frontend, Backend, Devops, AI

These are the areas most in demand in companies. Being a React or Vue developer. iOS or Android or knowing server configuration (usually Amazon, Google or Azure) and creating CI/CD pipelines.

You can learn all these skills on your own without going through a university degree and you can apply to a company with a good salary in a short time.

My recommendation is to start with Frontend with React, since you only need to learn Javascript, HTML, CSS and React fundamentals. Which can open the doors to mobile development with React Native.

But if you want something ultra power it can be _Elixir_ with _LiveView_. Although there you have to be better at selling yourself.

These are the alternatives to learn

- <https://www.freecodecamp.org/> (in English, free)
- <https://www.nextu.com/> (In Spanish, paid)
- <https://www.udemy.com/> (Multi-language, various courses of variable quality, paid)

Now if you want to complement this with basic sciences you can learn

- <https://es.khanacademy.org/> (Multi-language, free)
- <https://brilliant.org/> (English, paid)

Also if you want some other complementary course, with the same quality as at a prestigious University, you can go to <https://es.coursera.org/> where you can have free courses on diverse topics. You can also follow this roadmap <https://github.com/kamranahmedse/developer-roadmap>

Now if you want a specific React course and have some dollars in Paypal (You can use multi-caja to load dollars). These are two good courses. Use the code CHILELOVE for a significant discount.

- <https://reactforbeginners.com/>
- <https://advancedreact.com/>

You can also read some React tutorials here <https://alligator.io/react/>

A free course here <https://codingwithbasir.com/course/learn-react-redux/>

Nicolás Schürmann's courses are also good <https://www.nicolas-schurmann.com/cursos>

## Free Classes

What you can do if you want to breathe a bit of university air is sneak into classes. Believe me, if you go at the beginning of the semester at a University (normally a state one, private ones are a bit stricter) and talk to the professors, you can sneak into their classes for free. You can go to science classes or any subject that interests you. If you show real interest, the professors can admit you to their classes without problems.

## Studying Abroad

Many Latin American countries don't charge for studying at state universities. You can evaluate going to Argentina, Bolivia, Uruguay, among others. You would only have to pay for accommodation, transportation and food. Having a university degree will help you find work abroad, so you can get it abroad.

Also countries like Japan grant student visas for language studies.
Maybe studying an uncommon language like Japanese gives you opportunities
that other people don't have.

## Professional Technician

Although they are not as complete as an engineering degree. You can start with a technical degree related to the area. The important thing is that you are clear about the quality of the institution and the benefits you can get. Get advice and research the history and quality of professionals that come out of that institution, as well as the contents of the degree and its professors.

If you are going to study with debt, at least let it be a small debt that you can pay off in a short time.

The key here is to have a mindset of studying and making the most of the alma mater. Make the greatest effort possible to pass all the subjects on the first try and graduate in the shortest time possible. Many people enter the world of higher education with the mindset that it is a school where you can passively go to class and not have many responsibilities, skipping classes, not doing homework and wasting time in general.

If you are going to study in higher education, your mindset must be one of excellence, it must be superior.

